## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design CAPA from data governance, not just incident response.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model for evidence, artifacts, and policy checks
- Lifecycle state machine with DETECT → INVESTIGATE → CONTAIN → REMEDIATE → VERIFY → CLOSED
- Minimal REST API design for CAPA creation/update with linked traces
- Region-aware compliance metrics (violation rate, MTTR, FP rate)
- RCA template and canary rollout with automated data redaction

## Code Example
```javascript
// CAPA data model sketch (TypeScript)
type CAPARecord = {
  id: string;
  title: string;
  evidenceUrls: string[];
  policyChecks: string[];
  region: string;
  state: 'DETECTED'|'INVESTIGATING'|'CONTAINED'|'REMEDIATED'|'VERIFIED'|'CLOSED';
  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt: string;
};
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you surface policy checks in runtime dashboards?
- How would you automate data redaction in canary tests before broader rollout?

## Why This Is Asked

Covers a realistic data-leak risk from IAM misconfig in pipelines, focusing on formal CAPA processes at a beginner level.

## Key Concepts

- CAPA data modeling
- Lifecycle state machines
- Evidence-driven REST APIs
- Metrics for containment and prevention
- RCA templates and canary-based remediation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal Express route to POST /caps
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());
let caps = [];
app.post('/caps', (req, res) => {
  const cap = { id: Date.now().toString(), ...req.body };
  caps.push(cap);
  res.status(201).json(cap);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you secure the CAPA API (authorization, audit logs, idempotency)?
- How would you integrate with existing incident response tools?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft a CAPA program for cross-tenant data exposure caused by authorization cache mishandling. Emphasizes data modeling, lifecycle governance, API design, observability across regions, and rollback safety.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model with evidence, containment, remediation, and audit logs
- Region-aware, tenant-scoped metrics and dashboards
- Minimal REST API design for CAPA lifecycle
- RCA templates and controlled canary rollout for remediation
- Safe rollback and rollback criteria during canary

## Code Example
```javascript
interface CAPA {
  id: string;
  incidentId: string;
  tenantId: string;
  service: string;
  resource: string;
  evidence: string[];
  containmentActions: string[];
  remediation: string;
  status: "detected" | "triaged" | "contained" | "remediated" | "verified" | "closed";
  region?: string;
  createdAt: string;
  updatedAt: string;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate the canary rollout criteria and rollback?
- What logging/telemetry would you require to prove no similar exposure occurs post-remediation?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to design a beginner-level CAPA process for shared resources and governance, rather than implementing quick fixes alone.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model
- Lifecycle state machine
- Evidence-rich REST API
- Operational metrics
- RCA templates and canary remediation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Express API scaffolding for /caps
const express = require('express')
const app = express()
app.use(express.json())
let caps = []

app.post('/caps', (req, res) => {
  const cap = { id: Date.now().toString(), ...req.body }
  caps.push(cap)
  res.status(201).json(cap)
})

app.get('/caps', (req, res) => {
  res.json(caps)
})

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('CAPA service running'))
```

